From 7ea5d5c103c01b5a4f26ea48ec297dff2830c356 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 05:22:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lint: forbid plain file calls on SQLite -shm/-wal
 sidecars (check --only sqlite-sidecars)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

hermes-hub state.db was corrupted on 2026-11-11 because session_list_flight.data_stamp() read the
WAL-index header with open("<db>-shm") + close on every GET /api/sessions. POSIX cancels all of a
process's locks on a file when it closes any descriptor for it, and SQLite's WAL writer lock lives
on -shm, so a list call inside a write transaction let a second process write the same WAL
(fixed in a332e272cc7). The rule was documented in sqlite_safe_read but nothing enforced it:
92e3135eddb added that line with tests for the cache behaviour only.

scripts/check_sqlite_sidecar_opens.py is an AST guard, wired like check_detached_spawns
(scripts/check _GUARDS, so the hooks `scripts/check --install-hook` writes run it, and a lint.yml
step): open/FileIO, <x>.open/read_bytes/read_text/write_bytes/write_text and
copy/copy2/copyfile/copyfileobj/move (bare or on any receiver) are flagged when an argument (or
the receiver, unless it is os/io/shutil) carries a "-shm"/"-wal" literal, or a name assigned from one: variables per
function (assignment, loop/with target, parameter default), attributes and class constants by
name for the whole file, no flow. sqlite_safe_read.py (the lock-safe readers) is not scanned;
`sqlite-sidecar-open: ok — <why>` marks a call made with no connection open. On the tree before
a332e272cc7 it reports hermes_cli/session_list_flight.py:42, the incident line. The two other
hits are marked with their reason: kanban recover's corrupt-file fallback copy (its connection
is closed first) and session recovery's snapshot copy (inside offline_file_access, which refuses while a tracked
connection is open).

Not caught: a sidecar path built in another file or passed as a parameter, a subscript store, an
aliased open, and a plain open of the main database file.

Card: t_01a1c8d56207ac96
